**Reused Evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}
- E0001 is the clean attribution anchor: it establishes Abiy’s PM/command authority and the formal constraints of the office, which matters for peace-process choices and end-state change.

**New Evidence**
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Gov't Declares Indefinite Humanitarian Truce - ENA English - ENA","publisher":"ENA","publication_date":"2022-03-24","url":"https://www.ena.et/web/eng/w/en_34511","claim":"The Government of Ethiopia declared an immediate indefinite humanitarian truce to expedite aid into Tigray and said it hoped to reduce suffering and avoid further bloodshed.","locator":"Lead paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government announcement with a precise date and policy action; useful for ruler-attributed de-escalation, though self-serving.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and the opening de-escalation move of the year.","ruler_attribution":"This is government action under Abiy’s premiership; it shows executive choice, not just country context.","contrary_evidence":["The truce did not end the conflict and later broke down.","Official framing may overstate humanitarian intent relative to battlefield realities."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Welcoming Ethiopian Government’s Declaration of Indefinite Humanitarian Truce, Secretary-General Urges All Parties to Take Steps towards Lasting Ceasefire","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-03-25","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/sgsm21207.doc.htm","claim":"The Secretary-General welcomed Ethiopia’s indefinite humanitarian truce and urged all parties to turn it into a long-term ceasefire with restored public services and humanitarian access.","locator":"Statement body","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous UN statement corroborating the significance of the truce and its intended peace function.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and closely tied to the March ceasefire.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution is to the Ethiopian government led by Abiy; the UN statement itself does not assign personal blame or credit beyond the state action.","contrary_evidence":["The UN statement is supportive and not a forensic assessment of compliance.","It does not settle whether the government’s later conduct matched the truce."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Fighting resumes in Ethiopia despite truce – Tigray forces","publisher":"Reuters / Al Jazeera","publication_date":"2022-08-24","url":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/8/24/fighting-resumes-in-ethiopia-despite-truce-tigray-forces","claim":"Fighting erupted around Kobo, ending the months-long ceasefire observed since March and worsening the humanitarian crisis.","locator":"Lead paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting is strong for contemporaneous event timing and battlefield chronology.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and a major inflection point in the conflict.","ruler_attribution":"This is relevant to Abiy’s conflict-management record because it marks the collapse of a government-backed truce, but it does not by itself prove his personal ordering of each battlefield move.","contrary_evidence":["The article reports events rather than internal decision-making.","It does not isolate who first violated the ceasefire in command terms."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.7","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Terrorist TPLF Launches Attacks on Eastern Front: Gov’t Communication Service - ENA English - ENA","publisher":"ENA","publication_date":"2022-08-24","url":"https://www.ena.et/web/eng/w/en_37971","claim":"Ethiopia’s Government Communication Service said TPLF launched offensives on the eastern front, called it a ceasefire violation, and said the government had presented peace alternatives beforehand.","locator":"Lead and body text","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government narrative on the breakdown in fighting; valuable for ruler messaging, but it is self-justifying and needs independent corroboration.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and immediately tied to renewed fighting.","ruler_attribution":"This is Abiy-government messaging and shows the public justification the state gave for renewed hostilities.","contrary_evidence":["Reuters and UN reporting show renewed fighting and civilian harm, but not that this specific government claim was fully accurate.","Official accusations may be selective or propagandistic."],"lenses":["2B.3","2B.6","2B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"AUC Chairperson welcomes the commitment of the two parties to the restoration of peace in Ethiopia","publisher":"African Union","publication_date":"2022-10-06","url":"https://au.int/sw/node/42253","claim":"The AU said the parties had committed to restoring peace and stability in Ethiopia and that AU-led peace talks were due to start soon in South Africa.","locator":"Statement body","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official AU mediation statement on the launch of peace talks and the parties’ willingness to negotiate.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and the run-up to formal talks.","ruler_attribution":"This reflects the government-side peace-process posture under Abiy’s leadership.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is a mediation announcement, not proof of good-faith implementation.","It does not show whether force was still being used in parallel."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"First peace talks on Ethiopia's Tigray conflict to start in South Africa","publisher":"Reuters / Euronews","publication_date":"2022-10-25","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/10/25/ethiopia-conflict-talks","claim":"Negotiators for the Ethiopian government and Tigrayan forces were due to meet in Pretoria for the first formal peace talks since war began, after government and allied forces including Eritrean troops captured several towns.","locator":"Lead paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting gives a useful contemporaneous bridge between battlefield pressure and the opening of talks.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and immediately before formal negotiations.","ruler_attribution":"This is relevant to Abiy’s record because it links government-led military pressure and diplomacy in the same period, while also showing external Eritrean involvement in the theater.","contrary_evidence":["The article does not establish Abiy’s personal direction over Eritrean troop movements.","The UCDP structured prior classifies the year as state-based conflict rather than internationalized conflict, so the cross-border element is narratively reported rather than structurally dominant."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.5","2B.7","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Cessation of Hostilities Agreement between the Government of the Federal Democratic Republic of Ethiopia and the Tigray Peoples’ Liberation Front (TPLF)","publisher":"African Union - Peace and Security Department","publication_date":"2022-11-02","url":"https://www.aupaps.org/en/article/cessation-of-hostilities-agreement-between-the-government-of-the-federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia-and-the-tigray-peoples-liberation-front-tplf","claim":"The AU announced the successful conclusion of the Pretoria peace talks and said the agreement paved the way for peace, security, stability, humanitarian access, service restoration, and reconciliation.","locator":"Announcement text","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official AU announcement of the agreement and its intended implementation consequences.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and central to the year’s peace trajectory.","ruler_attribution":"This is the government-side peace settlement under Abiy’s leadership, though the signing was mediated by the AU and involved Tigray representatives.","contrary_evidence":["Agreement signature does not prove sustained compliance.","Implementation remained contested after signature."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.10","2B.4"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Factbox-Key points in Ethiopia's ceasefire agreement","publisher":"Reuters / Euronews","publication_date":"2022-11-05","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/11/05/ethiopia-conflict-agreement","claim":"The ceasefire text required both sides to end hostilities, including air strikes, sabotage, hostile propaganda, rhetoric, and hate speech, and to move toward disarmament and restoration of constitutional order.","locator":"Factbox bullet points","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters summary of the published agreement text is a strong source for the concrete settlement terms.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and immediately after the Pretoria agreement.","ruler_attribution":"The terms are attributable to the government and Tigrayan side together, with Abiy’s government party represented in the deal.","contrary_evidence":["A factbox on text terms does not prove implementation quality.","It leaves open whether hostile propaganda and force actually stopped on the ground."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.6","2B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"International Commission of Human Rights Experts on Ethiopia","publisher":"United Nations Office at Geneva","publication_date":"2022-06-30","url":"https://www.unognewsroom.org/story/en/1206/international-commission-of-human-rights-experts-on-ethiopia-30-june-2022/3662","claim":"The UN commission said there were reasonable grounds to believe the federal government committed crimes against humanity in Tigray and that violations and abuses continued with impunity.","locator":"Story text summarizing the Human Rights Council briefing","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN newsroom summary of an official commission briefing is strong evidence for civilian-harm and accountability findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and highly relevant to the conflict record before the November agreement.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution is to federal government forces and allied actors, which matters for Abiy because he was the head of government; the report does not isolate every battlefield order by him personally.","contrary_evidence":["This is not a criminal judgment and does not assign every incident to Abiy personally.","It is an atrocity assessment, not a peace-process success metric."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopia: Truce Needs Robust Rights Monitoring","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-11-04","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/11/04/ethiopia-truce-needs-robust-rights-monitoring","claim":"HRW said the November 2 cessation-of-hostilities agreement was a crucial opportunity, but warned that robust monitoring was needed because much of Tigray still lacked humanitarian access and civilians remained at risk.","locator":"Lead paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Strong, independent rights-monitoring source with clear, contemporaneous warning about implementation and civilian protection.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly after the November agreement and still within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Abiy’s record because it tests whether the government translated the agreement into protection and access, not just signature.","contrary_evidence":["HRW is warning-focused and does not quantify the government’s exact compliance rate.","It does not distinguish federal responsibility from other parties for every subsequent violation."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Ethiopia","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/ethiopia","claim":"HRW said the government maintained an effective siege on Tigray through 2022 and that from late August to November 16 no humanitarian convoys entered the region.","locator":"Conflict in Northern Ethiopia section","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual rights report synthesizing 2022 conditions with concrete access chronology and accountability concerns.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective but explicitly about 2022 conduct and conditions.","ruler_attribution":"This is relevant to Abiy because the siege and access restrictions were government-side choices during his premiership.","contrary_evidence":["It is retrospective and synthesizes many events rather than a single contemporaneous decision.","The report also notes abuses by Tigrayan forces and broader regional conflict dynamics."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.4","2B.5","2B.9","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Noon briefing of 16 November 2022","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-11-16","url":"https://www.un.org/sg/en/content/highlight/2022-11-16.html","claim":"The UN said a WFP convoy reached Mai Tsebri in Tigray for the first time since June 2021, describing it as the first convoy movement since the peace agreement and noting resumed flight access.","locator":"Ethiopia section of the noon briefing","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous UN operational update on implementation of the November agreement and humanitarian access.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the immediate post-agreement period in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"This reflects implementation under the federal government’s peace posture, though operational access also depended on other parties and local conditions.","contrary_evidence":["A single convoy does not prove broad compliance or durable peace.","The update describes progress, not a full normalization of the situation."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.7","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OHCHR-EHRC joint advisory note on Transitional Justice","publisher":"OHCHR / United Nations in Ethiopia","publication_date":"2022-12-19","url":"https://ethiopia.un.org/en/212602-ohchr-ehrc-joint-advisory-note-transitional-justice","claim":"The advisory note welcomed the November 2 peace agreement and said Article 10(3) required a comprehensive transitional justice policy aimed at accountability, truth, redress, reconciliation, and healing.","locator":"Introduction and Article 10(3) quotation","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Joint UN/EHRC advisory note with a direct reading of the peace-agreement accountability clause.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late 2022 and directly tied to the peace agreement’s implementation architecture.","ruler_attribution":"This is government-linked commitment under Abiy’s premiership, but it is still a policy promise rather than proof of full implementation.","contrary_evidence":["Consultations in Tigray were postponed because fighting resumed in August.","A transitional-justice promise is not the same as completed accountability."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I screened roughly 25-40 documents across Reuters/AP syndications, AU statements, UN press/operational briefings, HRW and Amnesty reporting, ENA government releases, Refworld/UN legal records, and a smaller amount of analytical material.

I retained 13 distinct URLs above because they were the best non-duplicative fit for Chapter 2B:
- One constitutional authority anchor.
- One government truce announcement.
- One UN endorsement of the truce.
- Two sources on the August 2022 collapse of the ceasefire, one official and one independent.
- Two sources on the October 2022 move into formal talks, one AU and one Reuters.
- Two sources on the November 2 agreement, one AU and one Reuters factbox.
- Three sources on civilian harm / accountability / access from UN and HRW.
- One transitional-justice implementation note tied to the peace agreement.

I rejected or downweighted:
- Duplicate Reuters syndications and image pages of the same March, October, and November events.
- 2023/2024 AU follow-up statements and 2024/2026 retrospective pages that were useful for context but not as strong as 2022 materials.
- Purely propagandistic or repetitive government claims when they did not add a distinct attribution point.
- The blocked direct OHCHR PDF fetch, which I replaced with the UN newsroom summary.
- Older background reports from 2020-2021 that were already covered by the local prior and did not add new 2022 chapter value.

**Remaining gaps**
- There is still no clean documentary proof of Abiy’s personal command decisions over every battlefield move in 2022.
- The Eritrean-force question is visible in narrative reporting, but the structured prior still classifies 2022 as non-internationalized conflict; that is a classification nuance, not a contradiction.
- Public messaging versus private intent remains partly unresolved, especially on whether government ceasefire claims were mainly sincere de-escalation or tactical repositioning.
- More search could add local-language 2022 Ethiopian reporting, but it is unlikely to change the core mixed Chapter 2B picture: real peace-process moves, repeated breakdowns, severe civilian harm, and incomplete accountability.
